WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.340
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.540
We're The Office Ladies.

8
00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:25.450
Oh, hi there.

9
00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:32.610
Okay, are you emotionally prepared because we're about to start breaking down goodbye, Michael?

10
00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:34.110
I can't lie to you.

11
00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:34.890
I am not ready.

12
00:00:35.290 --> 00:00:36.530
I am not ready.

13
00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:38.610
I don't think I'm ready either.

14
00:00:39.130 --> 00:00:42.010
This is Season 7, Episode 22.

15
00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:46.090
It was written by Greg Daniels, directed by Paul Feig.

16
00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:47.810
And you know what, Ange?

17
00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:50.110
I thought for this one, no summary.

18
00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:51.530
No summary.

19
00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:52.310
No summary.

20
00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:53.010
You know what?

21
00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:55.390
No summary needed is goodbye, Michael.

22
00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:56.910
It says it all in the title.

23
00:00:57.010 --> 00:00:57.290
Yeah.

24
00:00:57.610 --> 00:01:03.270
Listen, we're very excited because we have a very special guest to break down this episode with us.

25
00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:06.110
It is writer of this episode, Greg Daniels.

26
00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:14.570
Greg was with us in the studio for the whole episode, and he had so many amazing things to say that we decided we needed to make this a two-parter.

27
00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:15.270
Yes.

28
00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:20.670
Today, we will break down the first part of Goodbye, Michael with Greg Daniels, who, by the way,

29
00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:26.930
received Emmy and Writers Guild nominations for Outstanding Writing in a Comedy Series for this episode.

30
00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:27.970
So well-deserved.

31
00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:28.450
Oh, yeah.

32
00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:34.410
And maybe even more prestigious, Miles McNutt gave this episode an A.

33
00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:35.250
Come on!

34
00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:35.870
Yep.

35
00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:36.790
It's true.

36
00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:38.810
Well, Miles, I agree.

37
00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:44.090
Why don't we take a break, and when we come back, we'll start breaking down this episode with Greg.

38
00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:57.370
We are back, and we are with Greg Daniels.

39
00:01:57.490 --> 00:01:58.370
Greg is here!

40
00:01:58.670 --> 00:01:58.950
Hey!

41
00:01:59.510 --> 00:01:59.730
Yay!

42
00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:00.690
Thanks for having me.

43
00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:01.690
Hey!

44
00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:05.570
So let's just hop right in.

45
00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:07.330
Greg, this episode was written by you.

46
00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:09.130
It was directed by Paul Feig.

47
00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:15.370
Can you talk a little bit about how we got Paul back to direct this episode and why it was important to you to have him?

48
00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:17.010
Sure.

49
00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:17.930
Okay.

50
00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:30.670
So just to set the scene, I was very happy that Paul Lieberstein, who was running the show then, invited me back to write this because this was a big, key episode.

51
00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:34.190
And, you know, I had a lot of opinions.

52
00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:35.370
I really wanted to get into it.

53
00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:39.990
And so I'll just thank Paul for letting me do that.

54
00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:44.110
A lot of other folks would have been like, get rid of that old guy.

55
00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:46.170
I think I don't think so.

56
00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:46.990
It's hard to believe.

57
00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:49.090
So, all right.

58
00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:51.690
Well, anyway, I was very excited to write it.

59
00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:57.730
And the thing about Paul Feig, which was so great, you know, obviously you all know, but, you know,

60
00:02:58.130 --> 00:03:07.130
Paul was at that point probably the most trusted director since he had spent the whole year with everybody in season five.

61
00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:13.290
And, you know, there was a lot of emotional stuff happening with this.

62
00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:17.650
And I think it made people feel very safe to have Paul there.

63
00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:20.050
And, of course, he's also a brilliant director.

64
00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:23.690
You know, you bring out your big guns for a big episode.

65
00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:32.350
I do remember when we found out Paul was going to direct it that we were all just like, oh, thank goodness that we knew that he knew the show so well and us so well.

66
00:03:32.470 --> 00:03:34.490
I do remember feeling safe with that.

67
00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:35.070
Yeah.

68
00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:36.970
You know, there were some interesting things.

69
00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:38.910
I was rewatching the show and remembering stuff.

70
00:03:39.070 --> 00:03:51.690
And there was a strategy, which I thought was really smart that Paul had, of taking the most emotional scenes and putting it at the end of the, you know, of the shoot.

71
00:03:52.710 --> 00:03:56.510
Figuring that people would be pretty emotional by the time they got there and would overlap well.

72
00:03:56.510 --> 00:04:04.250
But I remember how difficult it was to shoot the most emotional scenes because it was getting way too emotional.

73
00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:06.030
You know what I mean?

74
00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:17.830
And it's like I always find it interesting with actors when they're auditioned because they're really comfortable doing something in front of thousands of people or in front of millions of people on TV.

75
00:04:17.830 --> 00:04:23.390
And then they get into an audition and sometimes they get all like seized up, you know.

76
00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:23.810
Yeah.

77
00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:27.050
And I think it's because they have actual personal stakes on this.

78
00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:28.090
It's not they're not pretending.

79
00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:30.130
It's like they want to get a job or whatever.

80
00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:32.110
And that's what happened here.

81
00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:36.030
It's like everybody had an actual personal relationship with Steve.

82
00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:39.130
That was, you know, and it was so hard.

83
00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:40.730
And I just remember, oh, yeah.

84
00:04:40.910 --> 00:04:41.390
Oh, my God.

85
00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:45.090
It was hard in the editing room sometimes to not have so much tears.

86
00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:52.330
Paul did an interview where he said one of the hardest parts about directing this episode was that people kept crying scenes with Steve.

87
00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:59.650
And he said he had to remind us, OK, you guys, you love Steve Carell, but the workers at Dunder Mifflin don't really like Michael.

88
00:05:00.070 --> 00:05:05.310
So you don't care if he leaves except for Jim, because Jim has a personal connection with him.

89
00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:08.110
And obviously, Dwight, who has affection for Michael.

90
00:05:08.350 --> 00:05:13.610
But Paul said, everybody, you need to act completely disinterested in this exit.

91
00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:15.810
And he said it was really hard.

92
00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:16.990
He had to keep telling us.

93
00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:17.630
Yeah.

94
00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:19.630
We had to separate reality.

95
00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:21.370
He had to give me a minute.

96
00:05:21.690 --> 00:05:23.390
He was like, Angela, do you need to take a minute?

97
00:05:23.870 --> 00:05:25.890
Because I kept crying.

98
00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:35.750
And, you know, I'm just showing Michael photos of me and the senator and the senator's aide, Thomas, you know, as we're going like to all these different things,

99
00:05:35.890 --> 00:05:37.310
antiquing and rollerblading and stuff.

100
00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:38.210
But I kept crying.

101
00:05:38.210 --> 00:05:42.110
Well, you were the most unlike your character, probably, of anybody on the show.

102
00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:46.790
So you were probably the most out of character at that moment.

103
00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:47.240
Yeah.

104
00:05:47.690 --> 00:05:50.210
I was like, I'm going to miss you.

105
00:05:50.830 --> 00:05:53.590
And they were like, OK, Angela Martin, rein it in.

106
00:05:54.370 --> 00:06:00.030
Well, before we get into the cold open of this episode, I dug into my digital clutter, Greg.

107
00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:02.690
You know I'm a digital hoarder.

108
00:06:03.170 --> 00:06:03.590
Trademark.

109
00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:04.510
Digital clutter.

110
00:06:04.870 --> 00:06:05.250
TM.

111
00:06:05.590 --> 00:06:06.250
Yeah, exactly.

112
00:06:06.250 --> 00:06:09.910
You should have your own theme song for Angela's Digital Clutter.

113
00:06:09.950 --> 00:06:10.350
I do.

114
00:06:10.450 --> 00:06:10.850
We do.

115
00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:11.370
Oh, my gosh.

116
00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:12.130
We have a sting.

117
00:06:12.350 --> 00:06:12.590
Do we have it?

118
00:06:12.690 --> 00:06:13.150
Oh, shoot.

119
00:06:15.470 --> 00:06:16.650
Angela's Digital Clutter.

120
00:06:17.950 --> 00:06:18.650
There it is.

121
00:06:18.670 --> 00:06:19.330
There it is.

122
00:06:20.390 --> 00:06:22.350
So I found two really fun things.

123
00:06:22.350 --> 00:06:27.230
I always like to see what we were emailing about, like what Jenna and I were talking about the week of an episode.

124
00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:32.490
And one I'm going to share now, but the other I'm going to save to the very end of the episode.

125
00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:34.190
So it's real special.

126
00:06:34.390 --> 00:06:35.330
So stay with us.

127
00:06:35.330 --> 00:06:37.030
Here's the first one.

128
00:06:37.210 --> 00:06:40.150
It's called Our Hip Jackets.

129
00:06:41.650 --> 00:06:47.950
And yeah, I emailed Jenna and Lee and I said, look at us and our hip jackets.

130
00:06:48.410 --> 00:06:52.950
We had gone to the table read and we had made a real effort, the table read for this episode.

131
00:06:53.270 --> 00:06:59.550
And Greg, there were a lot of guests at the table read and your wife, Suzanne, was one of them and she's sitting behind us.

132
00:06:59.850 --> 00:07:00.250
Cool.

133
00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:01.810
I'm going to show you a picture.

134
00:07:02.990 --> 00:07:05.430
And of course, you need to note our hip jackets.

135
00:07:05.890 --> 00:07:08.910
Was that a reference to Paul Feig's jacket wearing?

136
00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:11.610
I don't think so.

137
00:07:11.710 --> 00:07:11.950
I don't think so.

138
00:07:12.210 --> 00:07:14.170
They look like green army jackets.

139
00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:14.390
Oh, no.

140
00:07:14.390 --> 00:07:14.950
These are great.

141
00:07:15.130 --> 00:07:15.310
Yeah.

142
00:07:15.490 --> 00:07:15.990
Look at that.

143
00:07:16.130 --> 00:07:17.430
And then Suzanne is there.

144
00:07:17.730 --> 00:07:17.790
And then Suzanne's in the background.

145
00:07:18.190 --> 00:07:18.330
Yeah.

146
00:07:19.010 --> 00:07:19.570
Look at that.

147
00:07:19.570 --> 00:07:20.430
Oh, there's our trailer.

148
00:07:20.710 --> 00:07:21.370
Oh, my goodness.

149
00:07:21.590 --> 00:07:22.070
I know.

150
00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:25.310
Our rack for our TV set, our big bulky.

151
00:07:25.590 --> 00:07:28.650
Our big giant TV that we had to like scooch around.

152
00:07:29.190 --> 00:07:32.130
So that was my first item from my digital clutter.

153
00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:34.150
We read this episode.

154
00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:37.810
You and I wore hip jackets, Jenna, and we were real excited about it.

155
00:07:38.310 --> 00:07:39.170
I love it.

156
00:07:40.910 --> 00:07:43.130
Greg, this is everything you ever hoped for.

157
00:07:44.150 --> 00:07:45.630
I don't remember what I was wearing.

158
00:07:47.150 --> 00:07:49.110
Well, let's get into this cold open.

159
00:07:49.490 --> 00:07:54.610
This episode opens with Michael on the roof of the building in anticipation of his move to Colorado.

160
00:07:55.290 --> 00:07:58.150
Greg, this is how you described this scene in the script.

161
00:07:58.650 --> 00:08:00.170
You said, close up.

162
00:08:00.490 --> 00:08:03.610
Michael is in the sky, surrounded by clouds.

163
00:08:04.270 --> 00:08:09.450
Reveal he is sitting on a folding chair on top of an air conditioning housing on the highest point of the roof.

164
00:08:09.610 --> 00:08:11.290
He is wearing cowboy boots.

165
00:08:11.930 --> 00:08:17.630
There was an interview that Brent Forrester did where he shared that this was meant to symbolize death,

166
00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:21.730
that Michael was in heaven since this episode had the energy of a funeral.

167
00:08:22.410 --> 00:08:24.650
Do you remember sharing that with the writers?

168
00:08:24.890 --> 00:08:25.130
Sure.

169
00:08:25.810 --> 00:08:28.210
Actually, I mean, I don't know if it was.

170
00:08:28.210 --> 00:08:38.150
It was so much, like, dark about it, but it was also you could say you go to heaven and it's a beautiful ending to your time on earth.

171
00:08:38.150 --> 00:08:44.010
And he's going off with Holly to the top of a mountain in Colorado or something.

172
00:08:44.110 --> 00:08:44.250
Yeah.

173
00:08:44.250 --> 00:08:47.590
Yeah, so I would put a little bit more positive spin on it.

174
00:08:49.630 --> 00:08:50.110
Death.

175
00:08:51.150 --> 00:08:51.630
Funeral.

176
00:08:52.070 --> 00:08:52.130
Yeah.

177
00:08:52.790 --> 00:08:53.610
Goodbye, Michael.

178
00:08:54.070 --> 00:09:04.550
There is a lot of, I don't know what the term is, but, like, there's a lot of resonances in the script for different things to kind of get you in an emotional mood.

179
00:09:05.450 --> 00:09:08.330
Obviously, we did too much because we had to pull it back.

180
00:09:08.750 --> 00:09:18.810
But, yeah, little things like that that are just visual reminders of, you know, sort of, it's like a little bit of TV poetry.

181
00:09:19.130 --> 00:09:19.510
Yeah.

182
00:09:20.210 --> 00:09:21.770
Put them up in the, with the clouds.

183
00:09:21.770 --> 00:09:34.930
Dave Rogers shared that he loved that beautiful blue sky, but pretty quickly, the sun was too bright in Rain's eyes, so you had to put a white sort of, like, shield behind Steve.

184
00:09:35.290 --> 00:09:41.430
And so, in this scene, if you see blue sky, that's because that's before Rain's eyes started hurting.

185
00:09:41.710 --> 00:09:45.470
And then, then after that, you're going to just see white.

186
00:09:45.470 --> 00:09:47.330
What a baby Rain was.

187
00:09:48.850 --> 00:09:52.710
He refused to burn his eyeballs for this shot.

188
00:09:52.770 --> 00:09:54.990
He wouldn't look directly into the sun to say goodbye to Steve.

189
00:09:54.990 --> 00:09:55.590
Way to go.

190
00:09:56.030 --> 00:09:59.630
I have my first ever sound blanket catch.

191
00:10:00.190 --> 00:10:00.570
What?

192
00:10:00.690 --> 00:10:02.170
Does that have its own theme song, too?

193
00:10:02.230 --> 00:10:02.850
No, it doesn't.

194
00:10:04.550 --> 00:10:11.190
It's at 12 seconds on the roof floor next to Steve as he sits in the chair is a blue sound blanket.

195
00:10:11.410 --> 00:10:11.790
Good Lord.

196
00:10:13.050 --> 00:10:17.950
Oh, and, and my favorite line in the cold open is when Dwight says, where'd you hear that?

197
00:10:18.050 --> 00:10:19.470
Obvious XM radio.

198
00:10:20.590 --> 00:10:21.830
It's like such a great line.

199
00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:22.890
It rocks me up.

200
00:10:23.810 --> 00:10:23.950
Yeah.

201
00:10:24.110 --> 00:10:28.150
So, in this scene, Dwight appears and he offers Michael some Rocky Mountain oysters.

202
00:10:28.150 --> 00:10:30.270
It is a Colorado delicacy.

203
00:10:31.990 --> 00:10:34.590
Guys, I did a deep dive on the Rocky Mountain oyster.

204
00:10:34.850 --> 00:10:35.330
Woo-hoo!

205
00:10:35.970 --> 00:10:36.570
Are you ready?

206
00:10:36.830 --> 00:10:37.730
I am so ready.

207
00:10:38.710 --> 00:10:45.730
Rocky Mountain oysters are skinned bull testicles that are coated in flour, pepper, and salt and then fried.

208
00:10:46.490 --> 00:10:50.230
They are sometimes pounded flat before breading and frying.

209
00:10:51.390 --> 00:10:52.150
Flat balls?

210
00:10:52.390 --> 00:10:53.030
Flat balls.

211
00:10:53.170 --> 00:10:54.150
Do they have a different name?

212
00:10:54.310 --> 00:10:54.710
No.

213
00:10:54.870 --> 00:10:55.050
Oh.

214
00:10:55.270 --> 00:10:57.110
But in Canada, they have different names.

215
00:10:57.290 --> 00:10:57.430
Okay.

216
00:10:57.430 --> 00:11:00.130
In Canada, they are referred to as prairie oysters.

217
00:11:00.410 --> 00:11:01.790
That sounds smaller to me.

218
00:11:02.210 --> 00:11:03.710
Than a Rocky Mountain oyster.

219
00:11:03.930 --> 00:11:05.310
I think of like a prairie dog.

220
00:11:05.310 --> 00:11:08.430
Oh, like a giant prairie.

221
00:11:08.530 --> 00:11:08.870
Oh, yeah.

222
00:11:08.970 --> 00:11:09.470
I thought it was going to have the word moose in them.

223
00:11:09.470 --> 00:11:11.170
Or you get them from the prairie.

224
00:11:11.310 --> 00:11:11.990
Do what?

225
00:11:12.230 --> 00:11:16.330
I thought they were going to have moose oysters or something like that.

226
00:11:17.090 --> 00:11:20.830
I think they're almost exclusively a bull testicle.

227
00:11:21.010 --> 00:11:21.270
Okay.

228
00:11:21.630 --> 00:11:26.450
But in Oklahoma and the Texas panhandle, they are sometimes called calf fries.

229
00:11:26.450 --> 00:11:35.710
They've also been called cowboy caviar, Montana tender groins, dusted nuts, or swinging beef.

230
00:11:36.050 --> 00:11:37.290
Swinging beef?

231
00:11:37.710 --> 00:11:39.490
Where do they call it that?

232
00:11:40.150 --> 00:11:40.830
Around.

233
00:11:41.170 --> 00:11:41.830
Oh, my goodness.

234
00:11:41.910 --> 00:11:44.810
Nobody goes with the actual definition, I notice.

235
00:11:44.810 --> 00:11:48.050
Yes, no one just calls them bull testicles.

236
00:11:48.830 --> 00:11:57.350
They are mostly served at festivals, but they are available at Coors Field during Colorado Rockies games.

237
00:11:58.530 --> 00:12:00.690
They're considered an aphrodisiac.

238
00:12:00.910 --> 00:12:01.470
Really?

239
00:12:01.770 --> 00:12:02.170
Mm-hmm.

240
00:12:02.630 --> 00:12:03.550
Yes, that's right.

241
00:12:03.790 --> 00:12:07.190
Now, eating animal genitalia dates back to ancient Rome.

242
00:12:07.270 --> 00:12:08.190
Here we go, Greg.

243
00:12:08.450 --> 00:12:09.190
Here we go.

244
00:12:09.390 --> 00:12:10.550
Wait, where am I?

245
00:12:11.190 --> 00:12:12.070
Buckle in.

246
00:12:12.070 --> 00:12:14.710
We went from swing beef to ancient Rome.

247
00:12:14.710 --> 00:12:16.990
Turning to hardcore history here on this podcaSt.

248
00:12:17.190 --> 00:12:17.270
Yeah.

249
00:12:17.650 --> 00:12:23.790
Well, back in ancient Rome, it was believed that if you had like a problem with an organ in your body,

250
00:12:23.790 --> 00:12:31.330
if you ate the healthy version of that organ from an animal, then it would cure you.

251
00:12:31.550 --> 00:12:35.190
So if you had a bad liver, you could eat a healthy animal liver.

252
00:12:35.370 --> 00:12:40.330
If you were having, you know, impotence.

253
00:12:40.510 --> 00:12:41.470
You ate a penis?

254
00:12:41.470 --> 00:12:41.650
Yes.

255
00:12:41.810 --> 00:12:42.430
I don't know.

256
00:12:42.610 --> 00:12:44.370
Maybe that's when you eat the balls.

257
00:12:44.370 --> 00:12:45.790
I don't even know if I should be here.

258
00:12:48.170 --> 00:12:52.050
But Michael does say that he wants to put a salami in his pocket right afterwards.

259
00:12:52.450 --> 00:12:54.130
So maybe this is...

260
00:12:54.130 --> 00:12:56.950
See, this is all in line with what's happening.

261
00:12:57.430 --> 00:13:00.710
I hear you trying to move us along here, Greg.

262
00:13:00.710 --> 00:13:06.270
But I am determined to tell you this recipe that I found on whatscookingamerica.com.

263
00:13:06.330 --> 00:13:07.450
So she might be on like the website.

264
00:13:07.650 --> 00:13:12.470
So you just sort of throw to the website for people who are really interested and get the whole thing.

265
00:13:12.590 --> 00:13:17.730
I want people to have some of these tips for making Rocky Mountain oysters at home.

266
00:13:18.010 --> 00:13:18.270
Okay.

267
00:13:18.710 --> 00:13:19.430
Number one.

268
00:13:19.570 --> 00:13:20.990
Where do you get the ingredients, first of all?

269
00:13:21.650 --> 00:13:22.510
That's a good one.

270
00:13:22.510 --> 00:13:24.610
I don't know where you get the testicles.

271
00:13:24.870 --> 00:13:26.030
Well, you go to your bull guy.

272
00:13:26.390 --> 00:13:26.870
Yeah.

273
00:13:27.610 --> 00:13:29.490
Well, it is suggested.

274
00:13:29.550 --> 00:13:30.250
Number one tip.

275
00:13:30.570 --> 00:13:32.970
You're going to want to freeze the testicles firSt.

276
00:13:33.170 --> 00:13:37.150
Because then you can more easily peel the skin off while they're thawing.

277
00:13:37.250 --> 00:13:38.090
Very uncomfortable.

278
00:13:38.110 --> 00:13:40.050
This whole discussion is killing me.

279
00:13:40.050 --> 00:13:43.750
Number two, marinate them in beer for two hours.

280
00:13:44.710 --> 00:13:50.470
And then after you cook them up, serve them warm with a hot pepper sauce.

281
00:13:50.890 --> 00:13:59.210
And in case you were curious, a single raw bull testicle contains about 26 grams of protein, very little fat, and they are rich in zinc.

282
00:13:59.350 --> 00:14:00.510
So they're very good for you.

283
00:14:00.650 --> 00:14:01.390
Very good for you.

284
00:14:01.490 --> 00:14:01.970
Right.

285
00:14:02.290 --> 00:14:07.030
Well, I thought one of the funny jokes in this was that we didn't really put a lot of weight on.

286
00:14:07.030 --> 00:14:08.570
I hope people caught it.

287
00:14:08.690 --> 00:14:11.470
It was just Dwight munching away on them as he's giving his answers.

288
00:14:11.730 --> 00:14:11.780
Yes.

289
00:14:11.930 --> 00:14:12.710
I did notice.

290
00:14:12.970 --> 00:14:17.450
I looked at what he was eating, and it looked like chicken to me.

291
00:14:17.830 --> 00:14:18.670
A chicken nugget?

292
00:14:18.730 --> 00:14:20.850
It looked like a giant chicken nugget.

293
00:14:20.990 --> 00:14:24.250
I think the actual Rocky Mountain oysters do look like chicken nuggets.

294
00:14:24.250 --> 00:14:27.870
I can't imagine that Phil Shea fried up some Rocky Mountain oysters for Rain.

295
00:14:28.130 --> 00:14:29.230
He was very into authenticity.

296
00:14:29.230 --> 00:14:30.010
He was very thorough.

297
00:14:30.990 --> 00:14:32.030
We may never know.

298
00:14:32.170 --> 00:14:33.330
I didn't check into that.

299
00:14:33.470 --> 00:14:35.810
Rain looks like a guy who's had a lot of protein and zinc.

300
00:14:36.210 --> 00:14:36.870
He does.

301
00:14:37.030 --> 00:14:37.390
He's very healthy.

302
00:14:37.490 --> 00:14:38.370
He would eat it, too.

303
00:14:38.570 --> 00:14:39.070
He would eat it.

304
00:14:39.390 --> 00:14:41.270
Rain loved to eat food in scenes.

305
00:14:41.490 --> 00:14:42.170
He loved it.

306
00:14:42.330 --> 00:14:49.110
So whenever he had a chance to just completely chow down on something, I'm sure he was so tickled that he got to do that.

307
00:14:49.450 --> 00:14:56.870
As actors, have you guys ever heard the sort of criticism of actors that like to munch an apple during the scene?

308
00:14:57.210 --> 00:14:57.610
No.

309
00:14:57.710 --> 00:14:59.390
What is the criticism of eating an apple?

310
00:14:59.530 --> 00:14:59.710
Oh.

311
00:15:00.370 --> 00:15:02.990
Well, I don't know if it's just a sound department apple?

312
00:15:03.290 --> 00:15:07.830
No, it's just like it's a little bit of a crutch for an actor to be able to-

313
00:15:07.830 --> 00:15:08.590
Brad Pitt does it all the time.

314
00:15:08.670 --> 00:15:09.190
Does he really?

315
00:15:09.410 --> 00:15:09.670
Yes.

316
00:15:09.670 --> 00:15:11.050
He eats apples in movies?

317
00:15:11.250 --> 00:15:11.510
No, no, no.

318
00:15:11.570 --> 00:15:11.870
Food.

319
00:15:12.050 --> 00:15:13.570
Watch the Ocean's 12 movies.

320
00:15:13.590 --> 00:15:14.950
He's always eating something.

321
00:15:15.230 --> 00:15:15.490
Yeah.

322
00:15:15.490 --> 00:15:20.670
Kind of, it's like a way to appear very casual as you're giving your lines, you're munching away on stuff.

323
00:15:21.330 --> 00:15:21.490
Oh.

324
00:15:22.130 --> 00:15:22.310
Huh.

325
00:15:22.470 --> 00:15:23.690
Angela just threw Brad Pitt under the bus.

326
00:15:23.690 --> 00:15:24.890
I didn't mean to throw a chase.

327
00:15:25.350 --> 00:15:28.930
He's in front of actors eating and I just was thinking-

328
00:15:28.930 --> 00:15:29.470
I don't know if it's bad.

329
00:15:29.470 --> 00:15:30.570
It's just a thing.

330
00:15:30.710 --> 00:15:36.730
One of my favorite parts of like all of the Godfather movies and Goodfellas movies is how much people eat.

331
00:15:36.930 --> 00:15:38.570
The Sopranos eat all the time.

332
00:15:39.070 --> 00:15:39.750
They're always eating.

333
00:15:40.030 --> 00:15:40.350
Yeah.

334
00:15:40.470 --> 00:15:41.390
On the Sopranos.

335
00:15:41.910 --> 00:15:43.150
Sure, they have very good catering.

336
00:15:43.670 --> 00:15:48.370
Well, now we're in Michael's office and D'Angelo really wants one of Michael's toys.

337
00:15:48.650 --> 00:15:53.070
He wants the little 18-wheeler Dunder Mifflin-like paper delivery truck.

338
00:15:53.490 --> 00:15:57.950
Michael is clearly annoyed and he says, sure, take my favorite truck.

339
00:15:58.370 --> 00:15:59.690
D'Angelo says, you know what?

340
00:15:59.730 --> 00:16:00.590
I'm going to give you space.

341
00:16:00.590 --> 00:16:02.170
I'm going to post up in the break room.

342
00:16:02.310 --> 00:16:06.310
And then he has a talking head where he says, ugh, dead man walking.

343
00:16:07.110 --> 00:16:12.550
And Greg, I had to know if there was a candy bag alt for this talking head.

344
00:16:12.550 --> 00:16:17.470
And Greg just told me something outside, Jenna, that you didn't hear.

345
00:16:17.650 --> 00:16:19.170
And I can't wait for him to tell you.

346
00:16:19.330 --> 00:16:21.770
Will you tell us that you came up with?

347
00:16:22.010 --> 00:16:24.630
Oh, well, candy bag is my term.

348
00:16:25.090 --> 00:16:25.930
He made it up?

349
00:16:25.970 --> 00:16:27.170
You made up candy bag?

350
00:16:27.270 --> 00:16:28.910
Yes, yes, he did.

351
00:16:29.150 --> 00:16:35.710
I was trying to make it sound fun and exciting to the crew that they have to shoot extra material.

352
00:16:35.710 --> 00:16:38.030
We couldn't decide what the lines should be.

353
00:16:39.210 --> 00:16:40.010
So, yeah.

354
00:16:40.350 --> 00:16:42.710
Well, it's one of my absolute favorite things.

355
00:16:42.730 --> 00:16:44.150
And I didn't know you made up that term.

356
00:16:44.310 --> 00:16:45.290
And it makes me so happy.

357
00:16:45.490 --> 00:16:48.710
And there were candy bag alts for this D'Angelo talking head.

358
00:16:48.990 --> 00:16:50.950
I'm going to read you three of my favorites.

359
00:16:52.550 --> 00:16:52.950
Blech.

360
00:16:53.250 --> 00:16:55.090
Be professional for God's sake.

361
00:16:55.330 --> 00:16:56.150
Share your toys.

362
00:16:57.270 --> 00:16:57.850
That's good.

363
00:16:58.070 --> 00:16:58.590
I like that.

364
00:16:58.870 --> 00:16:59.390
Next one.

365
00:16:59.830 --> 00:17:02.670
I've been here three weeks and he hasn't touched the truck.

366
00:17:05.600 --> 00:17:08.180
And lastly, I don't even want the truck.

367
00:17:08.320 --> 00:17:09.920
I just want it because he has it.

368
00:17:10.080 --> 00:17:11.140
I'm a helicopter guy.

369
00:17:11.300 --> 00:17:12.160
I like helicopters.

370
00:17:12.520 --> 00:17:14.720
I kind of like them all better than what we use.

371
00:17:17.340 --> 00:17:19.240
My favorite is I've been here three weeks.

372
00:17:19.340 --> 00:17:20.620
He hasn't even touched the truck.

373
00:17:20.840 --> 00:17:20.980
Yeah.

374
00:17:21.540 --> 00:17:23.240
Well, you know what's interesting is this.

375
00:17:23.480 --> 00:17:28.180
I was looking over this and this was, you know, one of the reasons I really wanted to come in.

376
00:17:28.280 --> 00:17:29.720
I was very proud of this script.

377
00:17:30.120 --> 00:17:31.840
And, you know, it was an Emmy-nominated script.

378
00:17:31.840 --> 00:17:37.240
And I feel like I was sort of, you know, running on all cylinders at this point.

379
00:17:37.380 --> 00:17:45.720
And there's a lot of times in the script where there's little bits of information that are sort of snuck in that are setting up all these different storylines.

380
00:17:46.100 --> 00:17:53.240
And I'm sure the reason why we picked Dead Man Walking was because it led into Michael's next talking head where he says, I'm not sad.

381
00:17:53.240 --> 00:17:55.340
I don't have to leave till tomorrow and tomorrow I'll be a wreck.

382
00:17:55.340 --> 00:18:04.460
And that is setting up this whole notion of when is he actually leaving, which is Steve's idea.

383
00:18:04.600 --> 00:18:14.780
And it's like this wonderful way to disguise a lot of the emotional stuff so that people aren't having to talk to him directly as if it's his last day.

384
00:18:14.940 --> 00:18:16.980
They're just trying to, you know, have it be a normal day.

385
00:18:16.980 --> 00:18:17.680
Get through a normal day.

386
00:18:17.720 --> 00:18:18.020
Yeah.

387
00:18:18.020 --> 00:18:19.820
But he knows it's his last day.

388
00:18:20.520 --> 00:18:25.500
So that's probably why we picked Dead Man Walking instead of those other ones, which sound pretty hilarious.

389
00:18:26.840 --> 00:18:27.900
You're so right, though.

390
00:18:28.080 --> 00:18:34.540
And I mean, Jenna and I were talking about this as we prepped this episode about how thoughtful you were, Greg, in writing this.

391
00:18:34.740 --> 00:18:40.260
Because there are so many wonderful moments that point back to earlier episodes.

392
00:18:40.260 --> 00:18:46.460
And if you watch it with that in mind, I just got so much more emotional watching it.

393
00:18:47.460 --> 00:18:47.620
Yeah.

394
00:18:47.820 --> 00:18:51.700
I mean, I was so delighted to see that party planning committee scene.

395
00:18:51.840 --> 00:18:52.100
Yeah.

396
00:18:52.300 --> 00:18:58.600
And to see how differently Michael regarded the party planning committee in this episode compared to the first one.

397
00:18:58.720 --> 00:19:05.000
So much so that I pulled audio clips of both the first party planning and this one.

398
00:19:05.080 --> 00:19:06.560
And I thought we could listen to them back to back.

399
00:19:06.580 --> 00:19:07.400
Yeah, let's do it.

400
00:19:07.400 --> 00:19:07.600
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

401
00:19:07.660 --> 00:19:07.920
That's great.

402
00:19:11.240 --> 00:19:13.920
Green, yellow, red.

403
00:19:14.340 --> 00:19:15.020
How about green?

404
00:19:15.480 --> 00:19:17.420
I think green is kind of whorish.

405
00:19:18.020 --> 00:19:20.960
These are my party planning biatches.

406
00:19:21.220 --> 00:19:23.520
Pulled off an amazing 80s party last year.

407
00:19:23.780 --> 00:19:24.220
Off the hook.

408
00:19:25.100 --> 00:19:31.380
So I was thinking, if you haven't already gotten a cake, maybe go in for one of those ice cream cakes from Baskin Robbins.

409
00:19:31.480 --> 00:19:33.100
They're very good, very delicious.

410
00:19:33.460 --> 00:19:35.500
Meredith's allergic to dairy, so...

411
00:19:36.060 --> 00:19:38.000
She's not the only one that's going to be eating it, right?

412
00:19:38.360 --> 00:19:40.160
And I think everybody likes ice cream cake.

413
00:19:41.840 --> 00:19:43.980
That's just about her, so...

414
00:19:43.980 --> 00:19:44.540
It is.

415
00:19:44.800 --> 00:19:47.240
Her chocolate chip would be good.

416
00:19:47.520 --> 00:19:49.020
How about some...

417
00:19:50.180 --> 00:19:51.780
So that's the first one.

418
00:19:51.960 --> 00:19:52.220
Yeah.

419
00:19:52.520 --> 00:19:57.780
And now here is the final party planning committee scene with Michael, right?

420
00:19:59.240 --> 00:20:00.480
How about cupcakes?

421
00:20:01.240 --> 00:20:01.760
Please.

422
00:20:02.300 --> 00:20:03.480
What's wrong with cupcakes?

423
00:20:03.740 --> 00:20:03.900
Everything.

424
00:20:04.280 --> 00:20:05.040
There they are.

425
00:20:05.340 --> 00:20:07.140
Party planning committee together again.

426
00:20:07.800 --> 00:20:09.780
Well, we all wanted to plan your goodbye party.

427
00:20:10.100 --> 00:20:11.140
We thought this would be easier.

428
00:20:11.840 --> 00:20:12.600
We thought.

429
00:20:13.540 --> 00:20:15.100
We decided on the ice cream.

430
00:20:15.340 --> 00:20:16.940
Mint chocolate chip, your favorite.

431
00:20:17.380 --> 00:20:17.800
Mmm.

432
00:20:18.260 --> 00:20:19.620
Yeah, that was a surprise.

433
00:20:19.940 --> 00:20:20.420
You know what?

434
00:20:20.420 --> 00:20:22.840
I'm thinking maybe we should get ice cream that everybody will like.

435
00:20:23.180 --> 00:20:24.020
How about vanilla?

436
00:20:24.020 --> 00:20:24.960
Let's get vanilla.

437
00:20:26.500 --> 00:20:27.140
Okay.

438
00:20:27.500 --> 00:20:29.760
Tomorrow, I want everybody to have a good time.

439
00:20:30.240 --> 00:20:31.300
No drama.

440
00:20:31.720 --> 00:20:34.480
And as for today, just a typical day.

441
00:20:34.960 --> 00:20:35.640
All right?

442
00:20:36.680 --> 00:20:38.000
Should we get toppings?

443
00:20:38.460 --> 00:20:39.600
What do you like, Pam?

444
00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:40.460
What?

445
00:20:40.700 --> 00:20:42.380
What kind of topping would you like?

446
00:20:43.720 --> 00:20:44.300
Topping.

447
00:20:44.920 --> 00:20:45.860
Sounds good.

448
00:20:46.800 --> 00:20:47.660
Fudge it up.

449
00:20:49.700 --> 00:20:55.220
What I love about them is that the dynamic of the party planning committee has not changed.

450
00:20:55.500 --> 00:20:55.780
No.

451
00:20:56.060 --> 00:20:56.220
No.

452
00:20:56.480 --> 00:20:56.660
Yeah.

453
00:20:56.940 --> 00:21:01.440
We have grown not at all in our ability to work with one another.

454
00:21:01.820 --> 00:21:02.120
Yeah.

455
00:21:02.420 --> 00:21:05.100
I love how surprised you were at being asked your opinion.

456
00:21:05.380 --> 00:21:05.620
Yeah.

457
00:21:05.860 --> 00:21:07.200
That was such a good read.

458
00:21:07.900 --> 00:21:08.420
What?

459
00:21:10.160 --> 00:21:13.240
It's one of those things sometimes where you're afraid it's a trap.

460
00:21:13.580 --> 00:21:13.920
Yeah.

461
00:21:13.920 --> 00:21:14.160
Really?

462
00:21:14.240 --> 00:21:14.980
Where is this going?

463
00:21:15.200 --> 00:21:15.400
Yeah.

464
00:21:15.400 --> 00:21:16.440
I'm sort of scared.

465
00:21:16.440 --> 00:21:22.380
Well, I mean, there's another example of slipping in some good exposition there.

466
00:21:22.460 --> 00:21:25.220
Because he says, like, I don't want any drama today.

467
00:21:25.460 --> 00:21:26.620
Just a typical day.

468
00:21:26.820 --> 00:21:31.400
And that's, like, sets up his whole expectation for the episode.

469
00:21:31.400 --> 00:21:36.620
And then, you know, that lets us have an act break out of a very slender thing of, like,

470
00:21:36.980 --> 00:21:38.080
oh, no, I'm not leaving tomorrow.

471
00:21:38.080 --> 00:21:39.620
I'm leaving today at four or whatever.

472
00:21:39.800 --> 00:21:40.040
Yeah.

473
00:21:40.220 --> 00:21:40.360
Yeah.

474
00:21:40.480 --> 00:21:40.640
Yeah.

475
00:21:40.760 --> 00:21:42.860
I have a slightly cynical question.

476
00:21:43.080 --> 00:21:43.240
Yeah.

477
00:21:43.240 --> 00:21:48.760
Do you think Michael would have been more attached to the ice cream flavor if he knew he was going to be at the party?

478
00:21:49.140 --> 00:21:49.520
Yes.

479
00:21:49.760 --> 00:21:50.160
I think so.

480
00:21:50.160 --> 00:21:53.800
Is he able to be so generous because he's like, I'm not even going to be there.

481
00:21:53.960 --> 00:21:55.200
So whatever you want.

482
00:21:55.380 --> 00:21:57.100
I don't have to control it if I'm not eating it.

483
00:21:57.200 --> 00:21:57.420
Yeah.

484
00:21:57.540 --> 00:21:57.940
Exactly.

485
00:21:58.240 --> 00:21:58.520
Yeah.

486
00:21:58.560 --> 00:21:59.060
It's hilarious.

487
00:22:00.300 --> 00:22:01.960
Certain amount of growth, but not a ton.

488
00:22:03.140 --> 00:22:10.760
Well, after this scene, Michael's going to have a talking head where he talks about how he bought himself his world's best boss mug.

489
00:22:10.940 --> 00:22:16.140
But yesterday, the whole office gave him a world's best boss dundee.

490
00:22:16.320 --> 00:22:25.520
So he throws his mug in the trash, and then he sets his dundee on the desk, and it 100% mirrors that opening shot.

491
00:22:25.740 --> 00:22:27.280
How did you guys do that?

492
00:22:27.280 --> 00:22:28.300
It's so perfect.

493
00:22:28.520 --> 00:22:29.620
You wouldn't even know.

494
00:22:29.740 --> 00:22:31.940
It looks like it's like just copy and paste it in.

495
00:22:32.020 --> 00:22:38.560
I actually paused to see if you tricked us, and you actually cut away to the original shot, but you didn't.

496
00:22:38.820 --> 00:22:39.660
It's a new shot.

497
00:22:39.720 --> 00:22:40.540
It's a new shot.

498
00:22:40.680 --> 00:22:40.860
Yeah.

499
00:22:41.080 --> 00:22:44.460
Well, that was something that I added to the pilot was that mug.

500
00:22:44.600 --> 00:22:48.000
The fact that he bought himself his world's best boss mug.

501
00:22:48.980 --> 00:22:55.920
And, you know, when you're doing a pilot, you're always looking for, like, what is the one thing you can do that will encapsulate somebody's...

502
00:22:56.340 --> 00:22:56.700
Existence.

503
00:22:57.060 --> 00:22:57.420
Yeah.

504
00:22:57.740 --> 00:23:01.740
His complete psychology in one visual thing, you know.

505
00:23:01.740 --> 00:23:09.720
And so I always thought that that prop was, like, a really good, you know, example of somebody who had very little self-knowledge.

506
00:23:09.900 --> 00:23:15.580
And so it was good that he kind of outgrew that thing.

507
00:23:15.580 --> 00:23:18.060
And then it was a great episode, the Dundee episode.

508
00:23:18.060 --> 00:23:19.660
The previous one was great.

509
00:23:19.920 --> 00:23:22.480
And I can't remember when.

510
00:23:22.600 --> 00:23:27.620
I think it was just during the writing of it that I realized, like, oh, why don't we see that?

511
00:23:27.800 --> 00:23:37.100
Like, the use of documentary little conventions was very sparingly doled out for big moments, right?

512
00:23:37.200 --> 00:23:39.960
So, like, in this one, we pulled out some stops.

513
00:23:39.960 --> 00:23:41.360
And we did that.

514
00:23:41.420 --> 00:23:46.560
And we were like, cool, this is, you know, this is how that first shot came in.

515
00:23:46.560 --> 00:23:56.660
And, again, like, I think that that has a lot of emotional resonance because you realize, oh, my God, I've been watching that, you know, every week for the whole run of the show.

516
00:23:56.800 --> 00:23:58.600
And this is where it came in, the last episode.

517
00:23:58.800 --> 00:24:00.200
So, like, I thought that was cool.

518
00:24:00.360 --> 00:24:04.260
But then there's other stuff, like, when he, at the end, when he takes his mic off and stuff like that.

519
00:24:04.360 --> 00:24:10.420
And I feel like the taking the mic off was a big moment in the English show, I think.

520
00:24:10.580 --> 00:24:11.020
It was.

521
00:24:11.140 --> 00:24:16.360
There's a moment when Tim takes his microphone off when he's going to tell his feelings to Don.

522
00:24:16.560 --> 00:24:16.660
Yeah.

523
00:24:17.060 --> 00:24:20.080
And I think it's even in the Christmas special, if I'm correct.

524
00:24:20.360 --> 00:24:24.380
So, that was obviously, that's like a silver bullet that you get to use once.

525
00:24:24.720 --> 00:24:24.860
Yeah.

526
00:24:24.980 --> 00:24:26.040
And we saved it for this.

527
00:24:26.600 --> 00:24:29.980
Well, Michael is going to take out a list of employees' names.

528
00:24:30.060 --> 00:24:33.820
And you can see the list at five minutes and 32 seconds.

529
00:24:34.440 --> 00:24:37.660
And, Greg, did that list sort of shape the episode for you?

530
00:24:37.860 --> 00:24:39.220
Did you have an outline with that?

531
00:24:40.160 --> 00:24:42.420
Well, let me just remember this little story.

532
00:24:42.540 --> 00:24:47.100
Because I used to work on a show called The Wilton North Report.

533
00:24:47.820 --> 00:24:50.000
And Conan and I both worked there.

534
00:24:51.020 --> 00:24:56.740
It was a weird brief experience of a late night variety show.

535
00:24:56.740 --> 00:25:01.380
And they did have a cool documentary collective that was part of that show.

536
00:25:01.560 --> 00:25:12.340
And one thing I did once was take this documentary crew and go to this guy's retirement party who was retiring from the gas company, SoCal Gas.

537
00:25:12.340 --> 00:25:22.780
And we just shot it and kind of looked for something that would be touching or humorous about just sort of this average guy retiring from the gas company.

538
00:25:23.140 --> 00:25:29.600
And anyway, the mood of that informs this script for me.

539
00:25:29.640 --> 00:25:34.620
Because that guy was being toasted by people he'd worked with for a really long time.

540
00:25:34.620 --> 00:25:35.680
He was leaving.

541
00:25:35.900 --> 00:25:36.920
They gave him little gifts.

542
00:25:37.360 --> 00:25:39.200
You know, like, here's a golf ball.

543
00:25:39.300 --> 00:25:41.200
You can use it on your golf or whatever, you know.

544
00:25:41.400 --> 00:25:42.820
But it was almost sad.

545
00:25:42.940 --> 00:25:49.140
It was very sad because he was – they were almost packing him on a Viking ship and lighting him on fire and sending him off.

546
00:25:49.860 --> 00:25:53.580
It was like I don't think he was going to see any of these people again, you know.

547
00:25:53.700 --> 00:25:55.680
And it was very bittersweet.

548
00:25:56.100 --> 00:25:58.760
And so, I mean, I had that mood in the back of my head.

549
00:25:59.120 --> 00:26:01.380
And then when Michael got the list, he was going to go.

550
00:26:01.380 --> 00:26:08.580
Well, the thing about the list was it was kind of like the structure of the episode is he's going to say his goodbyes.

551
00:26:09.740 --> 00:26:13.860
And so, the list was a good roadmap for where we were going to go.

552
00:26:14.000 --> 00:26:17.260
And so, once you saw the list, you were like, okay, I know how this episode is going to go.

553
00:26:17.360 --> 00:26:21.280
I have a general feeling that he's going to have to cross off everybody on the liSt.

554
00:26:21.280 --> 00:26:28.700
And considering that the big sort of dilemma that he has at the end is he hasn't gotten to Pam, you know,

555
00:26:28.800 --> 00:26:34.860
to visually see the list and see everybody get crossed off except Pam is going to be our structure.

556
00:26:35.540 --> 00:26:37.480
So, that was very useful to have the liSt.

557
00:26:38.040 --> 00:26:41.460
It definitely reminded me of the Wizard of Oz a little bit.

558
00:26:41.580 --> 00:26:43.260
Yeah, there's a lot of Wizard of Oz in this.

559
00:26:43.480 --> 00:26:43.660
Yeah.

560
00:26:43.660 --> 00:26:51.880
Yeah, like, you know, it's like that final scene when they meet with the great Oz and he's like, you get a diploma and you get a medal.

561
00:26:52.100 --> 00:26:55.600
It's like Phyllis is shy and doesn't, you know, speak up for herself.

562
00:26:55.720 --> 00:26:57.260
She gets the clattering teeth.

563
00:26:57.480 --> 00:26:58.800
Yeah, totally.

564
00:26:59.060 --> 00:27:05.900
And I think he watched Wizard of Oz and saw himself as the Wizard of Oz maybe on some level.

565
00:27:05.900 --> 00:27:15.700
Yeah, I did catch a moment where I saw some real Michael growth because when he gives Stanley like the novelty pool table, Stanley says, it's got no balls.

566
00:27:15.800 --> 00:27:17.520
And Michael doesn't say anything.

567
00:27:17.800 --> 00:27:19.260
Yes, I noticed that too.

568
00:27:19.400 --> 00:27:20.240
He would have said that's what she said.

569
00:27:20.500 --> 00:27:20.680
Yeah.

570
00:27:21.080 --> 00:27:30.660
Well, also, I think the funny, that's also setting up this subplot in this episode is that Stanley got this little broken toy.

571
00:27:30.660 --> 00:27:35.120
Toy, and then Andy gets the 10 biggest clients right afterwards.

572
00:27:35.440 --> 00:27:36.260
And he's like, I'm going to lose them.

573
00:27:36.440 --> 00:27:37.300
I'm going to lose them.

574
00:27:37.380 --> 00:27:39.040
And then Michael's like, I have faith in you.

575
00:27:39.240 --> 00:27:43.160
And then later he shouts out in the middle of the scene, I just lost one.

576
00:27:43.520 --> 00:27:43.940
I just lost one.

577
00:27:44.020 --> 00:27:44.100
Border hardware.

578
00:27:44.360 --> 00:27:45.600
I just lost border hardware, Michael.

579
00:27:46.120 --> 00:27:46.480
Yes.

580
00:27:47.520 --> 00:27:47.860
Yeah.

581
00:27:48.580 --> 00:27:49.580
No, I love that.

582
00:27:49.660 --> 00:27:53.840
That was really, like, I was confident that was going to be a big laugh in writing it.

583
00:27:53.880 --> 00:27:55.940
I was like, I have him yell from across, you know.

584
00:27:56.280 --> 00:28:00.140
I also, I loved it on the show when there were stuff coming off Mike.

585
00:28:00.140 --> 00:28:01.200
You know what I mean?

586
00:28:01.200 --> 00:28:03.180
It just felt so real.

587
00:28:03.320 --> 00:28:05.380
But you're just like, oh, we're over here in accounting.

588
00:28:05.540 --> 00:28:05.820
Oh, no.

589
00:28:06.600 --> 00:28:09.720
He's going to lob this joke in from his B-plot.

590
00:28:10.140 --> 00:28:10.700
All right.

591
00:28:10.800 --> 00:28:12.380
I think we should take a break.

592
00:28:12.480 --> 00:28:15.240
And when we come back, keep breaking down the episode.

593
00:28:15.540 --> 00:28:15.880
Like it.

594
00:28:25.150 --> 00:28:27.590
We are back and we are still in accounting.

595
00:28:28.430 --> 00:28:32.470
Michael presents Kevin with a drawing of Kevin as a pig eating pizza.

596
00:28:32.870 --> 00:28:38.050
But then he tears up the picture and he says, don't be a caricature, Kevin.

597
00:28:38.630 --> 00:28:43.130
That's his gift to Kevin, I guess, is this, what, this lesson?

598
00:28:43.750 --> 00:28:47.010
Well, and Kevin says, I kind of am, like, good with myself.

599
00:28:47.010 --> 00:28:47.950
I like who I am.

600
00:28:48.010 --> 00:28:50.410
And Michael says, never settle for who you are.

601
00:28:51.570 --> 00:28:52.030
Yeah.

602
00:28:52.230 --> 00:28:56.910
So that's maybe more old-fashioned Michael giving some bad lessons out.

603
00:28:56.910 --> 00:28:58.730
But, I mean, I always did.

604
00:28:59.110 --> 00:29:09.310
I mean, Kevin was a kind of a pet peeve of mine a little bit with the writing staff because I was always trying to keep him three-dimensional.

605
00:29:09.650 --> 00:29:10.230
Do you know what I mean?

606
00:29:10.230 --> 00:29:14.870
And, like, I love that he was good at basketball in the basketball episode.

607
00:29:15.270 --> 00:29:16.150
Good at poker.

608
00:29:16.370 --> 00:29:16.570
Yeah.

609
00:29:16.630 --> 00:29:17.630
He's good at certain things.

610
00:29:17.790 --> 00:29:19.230
And, you know, he was sort of musical.

611
00:29:19.490 --> 00:29:23.330
And, you know, like there were other aspects to his character other than slow and fat.

612
00:29:25.230 --> 00:29:35.610
And, I mean, I think this was maybe something where I was enacting a conversation I had with the writing staff a lot through Michael's twisted, you know, vision of it.

613
00:29:35.670 --> 00:29:35.930
Right, right.

614
00:29:35.930 --> 00:29:37.730
He's trying to do something nice for Kevin.

615
00:29:38.810 --> 00:29:41.630
Phil Shea, by the way, really stepped up.

616
00:29:41.710 --> 00:29:44.230
There's a lot of really funny props in this episode.

617
00:29:44.550 --> 00:29:45.430
Like the scarecrow?

618
00:29:45.710 --> 00:29:46.190
Yes.

619
00:29:46.190 --> 00:29:47.410
I love the scarecrow.

620
00:29:47.630 --> 00:29:51.710
How many different scarecrows did Phil present on a silver tray?

621
00:29:51.930 --> 00:29:52.630
I think it was just this.

622
00:29:52.730 --> 00:29:53.670
He nailed it.

623
00:29:53.730 --> 00:29:54.350
He nailed it.

624
00:29:54.410 --> 00:29:54.850
Yeah.

625
00:29:55.050 --> 00:29:56.430
He completely nailed it.

626
00:29:56.530 --> 00:29:58.630
I loved doing props with him.

627
00:29:58.630 --> 00:30:06.090
Like there's, I can't remember which episode, but there's like a little Venus of Willendorf statue, you know?

628
00:30:06.350 --> 00:30:06.850
Oh, yes.

629
00:30:07.030 --> 00:30:15.310
That's sort of like Neanderthal pregnant carving that I think Mose has or Dwight has or something in his farm.

